WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
Hey guys, this is Jared, one of the creators here at Wisecrack.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.066
Today we’re talking about one of the best shows on TV right now and a personal favorite: Rick and Morty.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.933
We know you’re probably jonesing for some new episodes after the year-and-a-half wait was announced...

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
so we wanted to hold you over with an analysis 
of Rick and Morty so far —

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.466
the philosophy, the science, and everything you missed while you were laughing at the dick jokes.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.333
Rick and Morty chronicles the exploits of a mad scientist and his naive grandson

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.266
as they go on deeply irresponsible and awesome space adventures.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.533
It’s more or less the plot of "Back to the Future",

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.066
but "Doc" is a gassy alcoholic,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.333
"Marty", er-"Morty" never tries to hit on his mom,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
and the duo travels through alternate dimensions rather than time.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.866
Rick and Morty is deeply rooted in the traditions of sci-fi horror, often paying homage to things like:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
"Ghostbusters,"

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
filmmaker David Cronenberg,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
"Nosferatu"

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
and Freddy Kreuger to name a few.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
The show even references "Zardoz," a camp classic and guilty pleasure of mine.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.933
Nowhere else will you get to see Sean Connery in a futuristic space thong.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.933
But seriously. See the movie.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.933
It’s actually really smart.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
While the show pays homage to a wide spectrum
of sci-fi and horror,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.533
it has an affinity for a particular sci-fi tradition: cosmic horror.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
Pioneered by literary horror legend H.P. Lovecraft,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.333
the cosmic horror genre emphasizes the terror of that which is outside our grasp to comprehend.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.333
Like Lovecraft, Rick and Morty uses the cosmos as a way to explore deep philosophical questions

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.866
by imagining what lies hidden in
the black depths of space.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.733
The show opening even makes a visual reference
to Lovecraft’s Cthulhu.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.533
Cosmic horror goes way beyond just cheap jump-scares;

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
it presents us with the vast unknown and unimaginable.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.466
That simultaneous feeling of fright, disgust,
and dread that we feel

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.866
as whatever lurks in incomprehensible space suddenly
comes crashing into our own reality.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.666
Rick’s portal gun is constantly confronting us with this unknown —

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.466
as he jumps head-first into other realities, the results are sometimes horrifying,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.333
sometimes hilarious,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.266
and sometimes both.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
Occasionally, that reality comes to us.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.200
Particularly, in the episode “Get Schwifty.”

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.333
Earth is approached by a giant space-head whose sheer gravitational mass causes mayhem on Earth’s ecosystem.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.066
The giant head commands the entirety
of Earth to show him what they’ve got.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.266
A cult soon develops to worship this cosmic entity,
and Rick, realizing the head is a Cromulon,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.466
informs the president that they have to come up with a hit song and win the intergalactic equivalent of "America's Got Talent,"

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.866
or be disintegrated by a giant space laser.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Giant floating cosmic entities aren’t anything
new in the science fiction universe.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.666
Our old friend H.P. Lovecraft used the idea constantly,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.533
developing a race of “Great Old Ones” who he describes in books like “The Call of Cthulhu” and “The Dunwich Horror.”

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.133
There are a few interesting questions that
big floating scary things bring up.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
First, cosmic horror begs the question:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.266
Are we significant?

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.333
Most science fiction is convinced that humanity is, metaphorically, the center of the universe.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Whether we’re battling the empire,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
or trying to get all the aliens to just be friends,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.666
humanity — or, things that look like humanity — take center stage.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.733
Cosmic horror inverts that premise
and asks the question:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.466
What if the universe doesn’t give a shit about us?

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.333
For the Cromulons, humanity is merely 
an object of entertainment.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.533
Annihilating a planet is not an act of spite or a cause for concern, it’s just something you do.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.133
Cosmic horror is full of these giant entities that demonstrate just how insignificant humanity actually is.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
And that’s what is really terrifying.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.266
Or, in the case of Rick and Morty,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.533
kind of hilarious in a dark, dark way.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
In the book “In the Dust of This Planet,”

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.666
Contemporary American Philosopher Eugene Thacker describes this “Cosmic Pessimism” prevalent
in the horror genre

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.866
that imagines a “world-without-us” and thinks through the universe as

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.333
“absolutely unhuman, and indifferent to the hopes, desires and struggles of human individuals and groups.”

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
The show embodies this cosmic pessimism 
almost perfectly.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
Rick and Morty even die,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.333
only to be replaced by one of the infinite other versions
of themselves in an alternate reality.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
Not only are we but a speck of dust on a tiny blue marble in the cosmos at large,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.066
we’re also like one of an infinite amount of the exact same speck of dust.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.733
Rick and Morty doesn’t want us to be terrified
of our own insignificance, it want us to laugh at it.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.133
Even though we are but specks of dust 
to these cosmic horrors,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.733
we, too, are the cosmic horrors to other specks of dust.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
In “The Ricks Must Be Crazy,”

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.333
we learn that Rick’s car battery is powered by a micro-universe

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
whose sole purpose is to perform an inane task: produce energy.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
And, in that universe, they invent another mini-universe.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
And so on, and so on.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.266
Rick might as well be one of Lovecraft’s cosmic deities to the denizens of the micro-verses.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
It’s like cosmic inception, brah.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
In a multiverse beyond our comprehension, with
infinite possibilities,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
values and meanings start to slip away.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
All that can remain is pessimism.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.133
Which brings us to our next section, on the Existentialism of Rick and Morty.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.066
Rick and Morty addresses the question of our very existence in almost every single episode.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.866
On the one hand, we have the cosmic insignificance
of humanity as a whole,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.266
and on the other we have the frivolity of individual human life.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.600
The show offers us two examples of how to respond to our pathetic existence through Rick and Jerry.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
First, we have Jerry,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
who is seemingly oblivious to the soul-crushing mediocrity and pointlessness of his own life.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.466
He is literally more happy in a poor simulation of the world than his actual life.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.466
Or, as Arthur Schopenhauer might
well have described Jerry’s life:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.266
Jerry is a failure.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
He’s unemployed,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.266
his wife hates him,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.533
and he’s not exactly the sharpest tool in the shed.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.066
But one can argue that Jerry is better off than the belligerently drunk Rick

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
because he’s simply too dumb to realize that his life is pointless.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.333
But in this ignorance, Jerry seems content toiling away for the sake of nothing.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.266
Then there’s Rick, who understands the
meaninglessness of life and accepts it.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
But despite Rick’s sarcasm and nonchalant attitude
towards pain and suffering...

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.133
...he’s also an alcoholic whose catchphrase means 
“I am in great pain.”

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.133
The pure rationalism of the universe furthers
the feeling of insignificance of our own lives.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.866
We’re left with a paradox.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.066
Science allows us to make some sort of 
sense of the universe through formulas and theorems,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
but we as humans are left confronting the bleak, arbitrary nature of our own existence.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.733
Science can reason away any sense of sanctity or emotion

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
and turn the human experience into something meaningless.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.533
Rick takes time out of avoiding his own pending death to explain, mathematically, that Summer and Morty are both pieces of shit.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.733
Rick goes on to rationalize away his feelings for Morty,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.666
explaining that their bond is only an easy way 
to hide Rick’s genius brain waves...

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
...from the intergalactic po-po.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
In another episode, Morty, as Roy, 
grows up and dies in a video game.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
After losing, he briefly forgets his own existence.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.733
The audience is left to question what exactly separates the fake reality of the “Roy” game to his own existence.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.333
After all, if Morty remembers his “fake” life,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.666
and experiences all the emotions and sensations
that constitute our own consciousness,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
what’s the difference?

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.266
Rick and Morty is constantly playing with this dichotomy between meaningless simulations and our actual life.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.933
If we recoil from the idea of living a “fake”
life inside a video game,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.466
it’s because we ascribe some meaning to the authenticity of our own waking life.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.466
But Rick and Morty is quick to display for all that that is a giant crock of shit.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.866
What’s the difference between killing the memory parasites whom the family feels genuine emotion for,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
and shooting, say,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
Mr. Poopybutthole?

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.933
This crisis of values, of our own meaning
in life, is taken up by tons of existentialists.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.666
But our old friend, Friedrich Nietzsche, is 
particularly helpful here.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.866
Nietzsche tells the parable of the madman, who comes into town screaming,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.866
“God is dead!..And we have killed him!”

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.133
Now, what Nietzsche was really trying to say is that after the Enlightenment and the scientific revolution,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.133
the big being in the sky that gave value to our lives 
was no longer relevant.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
After the death of God in philosophy, 
what remains is nihilism.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
A void of meaning.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.533
This struggle over the lack of meaning 
is what defines Rick Sanchez.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
Rick uses science to demystify everything.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.733
Love is just a set of chemical reactions.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.533
He even beats the devil, and, in a way

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
actively plays out the death of God.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
We’re constantly left wondering: what does Rick care about, if anything at all?

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
Rick’s character embodies a tension between a kind of active and passive nihilism.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.466
The passive nihilist, for Nietzsche, is resigned to the hopelessness and meaninglessness of life.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.733
The active nihilist, however, actively seeks to destroy the old values and start anew.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
It’s clear that something makes Rick tick, we’re just not sure what.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
After all, he’s labeled as an intergalactic terrorist
fighting for something,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
and eventually he gives himself up to save his family.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.666
Rick’s actions may seem paradoxical if only because he himself is struggling to make meaning out of the universe.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.533
The show also does its part to interrogate
our own sense of morality,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.666
refusing to draw clear lines between right and wrong or good and evil.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
Morty’s attempts to be a do-gooder 
are always thwarted.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.133
After freeing a group of people 
from the body-snatching Unity,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.200
a nipple-driven race war commences between the
now-freed rabble of pedophiles and prostitutes.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.333
When confronted with the grim consequences, Summer aptly replies:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
“I didn’t know freedom meant people 
doing stuff that sucks.”

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.733
Herein lies another existential question:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
What is biological life without “living?”

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.466
If we, the audience, are repulsed by the 
thought of body-snatchers,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.466
the show also makes us consider the fact that our irrational attachment to freedom

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
is what allows us to live in a world
that is oh-so-terribly fucked up.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.800
Another way to look at the existential question in Rick and Morty is through the work of Albert Camus,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.600
specifically in "Meeseeks and Destroy".

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.533
The central tenant of Camus’ “absurdism” is that life consists of two irreconcilable facets:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.800
The human tendency to find meaning in life

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.933
and the universe’s complete indifference
to our existence.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
In Rick and Morty, we see the opposite of this in the Meeseeks:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.666
“Meeseeks are not born into this world 
fumbling for meaning, Jerry!"

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.933
"We are created to serve a singular purpose"

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.266
"for which we will go to any lengths to fulfill!"

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
For Camus, human life was exactly the opposite, as illustrated in the myth of Sisyphus,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
the king who was sentenced to spend an eternity pushing a boulder up a hill

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.066
only for it to fall back down 
upon completion.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
Over and over, again.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.733
Or, for Jerry, it’s not a boulder, but his golf swing.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.666
And then, there’s this:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
Many characters in the show, rather than seek meaning for their own existence,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.200
simply resent their creators for bringing them 
into this world.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.933
We see this "Frankenstein-complex" 
not only in the Meeseeks

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
but in Abradolf Lincler:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.066
Morty Jr, and of course, Frankenstein's monster himself makes an appearance.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.933
For Camus, we are perpetually frustrated 
 by our floundering attempt to find meaning and purpose in life

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
and our inability to do so.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.600
He offers, as an alternative, to embrace the emptiness of the absurd.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.133
Or, as Rick says:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.533
"The answer is: Don't think about it."

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.266
Or, to quote Morty:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
"Nobody exists on purpose."

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.733
"Nobody belongs anywhere."

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.600
"Everybody's gonna die."

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.066
"Come watch TV?"

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.266
The fictional and sometimes almost-real science
of Rick and Morty

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.533
allows us to explore all of the ways that, despite our love of Rick,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.133
our lives are as sad and pathetic as Jerry’s.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.266
But the science in the show also works as
an excuse for some really nerdy jokes.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.533
Some are obvious, like the entire episode 
 loosely based off the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.400
This principle from quantum mechanics, pioneered by

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
a notable meth dealer

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.466
states that it is impossible to know both 
the location and momentum of a particle.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.733
That’s because the tiny objects that make up our universe are at the same time waves and particles.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.933
If that’s confusing, it’s because it is.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.666
Rick and Morty draws humor from a 
superficial reading of science here,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.933
claiming that this uncertainty of quantum mechanics can cause our own realities to split.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.866
Outside, a sea of Schrödinger's cats are floating around,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.333
a reference to the 1935 thought experiment 
by Erwin Schrödinger.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.866
It ponders: in a quantum world where particles can be two things at once,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.400
is it possible for a cat rigged with an elaborate quantum
booby-trap to be both alive and dead at the same time?

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Because...

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.533
Austrians have dark, dark
imaginations.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.600
This tenuous relationship to scientific truth could be viewed as a jab at the science fiction genre

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.466
for constantly inventing scientific mumbo-jumbo to justify unlikely plot points...

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.133
...like stupid and smart brain-waves 
canceling each other out.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.933
In another episode, Rick is seen traversing
tiny climates on a tiny planet,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
which is...

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.733
not at all how that works.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.666
In same episode, the show’s creators poke fun
at the trust we place in TV narratives

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.733
by making a joke about forced perspective

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.133
The show also exhibits a unique kind of atheism

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.533
that constantly invalidates religious figures by boiling them down to pure science.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.600
It also acknowledges the frequent misuse of science by those with religious agendas.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.666
In “Something Ricked This Way Comes”

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.266
Jerry’s “personal” interpretation of science leads him on a crusade to reinstate Pluto’s planet-hood.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.400
He is greeted by the Plutonians, who have not-so-subtle crosses in their eyes —

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.266
drawing a comparison to religious fundamentalism.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.533
Of course, it’s all a ploy to manipulate Plutonians

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.733
into thinking the destruction of their planet by their corporate overlords is totally not a big deal.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Hmm...

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.866
So, to pull it all together,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
Rick and Morty is a show that holds nothing as sacred.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.266
By exploring the absurdities of a purely rational universe and pushing the boundaries of decency in a sci-fi horror setting,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.866
we get to ask all sorts of fun, depressing questions like:

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.333
“Why am I here?”

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
and

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.333
“What’s the point of it all?”

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.733
We’re not really sure where the show will go 
with Rick’s character.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.333
Will he finally do something for the greater good,

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.933
or is his latest move just another bleak self-serving ploy?

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.066
As always, thanks for watching.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.146
Wubbalubbadubduuuuub!

00:00:00.146 --> 00:00:02.066
We hope you're liking these special episodes.

